{"calculus_ref":"stdo://releases/v2.5.1-rc.2/standards/AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS.md","clause_relations":[{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-typed-lineage","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-causal-claim","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"Three graph families stay distinct: G_id, the identity-dependency DAG; G_occ_cause, the declared occurrence-causation DAG; G_evt_cause, the declared event-causation DAG. Each is acyclic under its own typed relation. Cause is established only by an admitted causal claim under competent authority and evidence. Timestamp, ordinal, adjacency, containment, a common subject, a shared actor, correlation and arrival order do not create cause.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE, Causality and typed lineage; P6, P3","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:requires","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:causal-families-stay-distinct-and-cause-is-admitted"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:refinement-construction-availability","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:result-algebra","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:target"},{"literal":"candidate_ready -> satisfied; refused -> falsified; incomplete -> indeterminate; re_entry_requested -> out_of_frame or invalid_basis.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:refinements-map-totally-to-core","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:support"}],"clause_type":"relation","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:maps-to","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:construction-availability-maps-to-core"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-typed-lineage","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-framework-event","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"Lineage relation kinds of the closed RelationKind population (kind: source -> target; core law): identity_depends_on: ProfileRecordRef -> ProfileRecordRef; earlier identity input; acyclic. causally_precedes_occurrence: Occurrence -> Occurrence; admitted material cause; acyclic. causally_precedes_event: FrameworkEvent -> FrameworkEvent; admitted material cause; acyclic. supports_event: FrameworkEvent -> FrameworkEvent; typed evidential lineage; not cause by implication. corrects_event: FrameworkEvent -> FrameworkEvent; typed correction lineage; does not erase its target. component_of_occurrence: Occurrence -> Occurrence; child application belongs to one declared composite application. event_for: FrameworkEvent -> Occurrence; optional and permitted only by the event's declared scope law. Each relation is an exact TypedRelation; a ref outside the closed union, resolved under a wrong population or sort, or absent from the applicable declared external domain refuses.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE, Closed relation families (lineage rows only); P3, P4a, P5","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:distinguishes","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:lineage-relation-kinds-are-typed"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-typed-lineage","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"OP-001: the directed graph formed by identity_depends_on is finite and acyclic. Every dependency target exists before the dependent identity is minted. A future result or event cannot be smuggled into its own occurrence seed.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE OP-001; P3","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:forbids","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:op-001-seed-acyclicity"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-semantic-cut","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-framework-event","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"OP-002: for a relation claim, one admitted semantic cut contains the unchanged claim, its admission judgment, the event and its scope and admission judgment, the deterministic materialised relation, the exact source frontier, and the unique successor frontier. The source frontier that the claim, the event and the cut name is one identical frontier, and the successor equals that frontier plus exactly the admitted event. Missing, conflicting or inconsistent members refuse the cut. This atomicity is semantic; no storage or transaction mechanism is prescribed.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE OP-002; P3, P4b, P6","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:requires","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:op-002-atomic-semantic-admission"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-framework-event","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"OP-003: corrects_event(new, existing) keeps both immutable events. A correction changes later projection through an explicit selection law; it does not delete, rewrite or retrospectively re-identify the corrected fact.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE OP-003; P3, P4b","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:forbids","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:op-003-correction-without-erasure"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-precedence-coordinate","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:authority","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"OP-004: a projection may use a declared precedence coordinate to select current truth. The coordinate, its comparison law, its basis and its admitting authority are explicit. The coordinate orders eligible facts; it gains no semantic, admission, causal or decision authority.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE OP-004; P6, P3","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:forbids","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:op-004-precedence-is-not-authority"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-mutable-subject","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"OP-005: W remains the carrier of mutable reality. Observation, evidence, result, checkpoint, event, projection, cache or model content may describe W; none replaces it. A later traversal application observes the then-current W under the stable binding when authority is unchanged. Snapshotting or rollback may be supplied by a separately authorised application; this profile neither requires nor forbids it and does not infer rollback from a new occurrence.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE OP-005; P3, P0, P6","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:preserves","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:op-005-mutable-reality-conservation"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-mutable-subject","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"OP-006: iteration does not mutate an earlier occurrence. Each traversal reapplication mints a fresh immutable occurrence over the evolving subject, so the admitted occurrence and event history is append-only even when the external subject changes repeatedly.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE OP-006; P3, P4b","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:preserves","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:op-006-immutable-iteration-history"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-framework-event","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"OP-007: every event carries its declared scope class. event_for is present only when the scope law names one exact occurrence. Facts that are subject-wide, publication-wide or otherwise independent of any occurrence stay independent.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE OP-007; P3","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:requires","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:op-007-scope-conservation"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:refinement-construction-availability","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:refinement-disposition","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:refinement-ambiguity-outcome","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:refinement-profile-closure","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"A declared refinement answers a different question, keeps its own vocabulary and declares a total mapping to the core; no refinement adds a core member.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:requires","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:refinements-map-totally-to-core"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:traversal-structural-refusal","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:traversal-stop-kind","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"A structural refusal is one whose source is the profile's closed structure rather than a row of the constraint table: an unknown or missing required field under the closed value contract; a missing population, hidden record, ambiguous ref, unknown record kind or undeclared field domain; a missing row, target outside its row, wrong population or sort, unresolved external target, cardinality violation, basis-relation mismatch or nested coordinate or witness defect refused at model validation; a qualifier-record defect refused at structural admission; and a reference that fails AC-003. Stop refusal and decision refused from such a source map to invalid_basis. Falsified for partial and partial_effect does not record that the subject changed: the partial_effect residual and its re-entry carry that fact and survive every join; the core member alone never licenses treating the effect as absent.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE, Closed constraint, judgment, residual and stop kinds; P5, P6, P4b","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:distinguishes","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:structural-refusal-and-partial-effect-losses"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:traversal-stop-kind","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:traversal-judgment-decision","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"The core stop kinds are hold, gap, refusal, invalid_basis, out_of_scope and partial_effect. Every constraint predicate returns exactly one of satisfied, falsified, indeterminate or invalid_basis. satisfied maps to the judgment's positive decision, the first decision it lists; invalid_basis maps only to invalid_basis; the constraint table fixes every other mapping. An implementation cannot turn an omitted result, a residual or a stop into success.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE, Closed constraint, judgment, residual and stop kinds; P5, P6","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:requires","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:traversal-stop-kinds-and-constraint-results"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:traversal-stop-kind","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:traversal-judgment-decision","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:refinement-profile-closure","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:result-algebra","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:target"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:r-satisfied","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:target"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:r-falsified","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:target"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:r-indeterminate","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:target"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:r-out-of-frame","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:target"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:r-invalid-basis","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:target"},{"literal":"Default total mapping to the five core members, adding none (ACTIVATION §3 one-algebra rule; AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS §5 requires a traversal's stop algebra to map totally). Each value, read with the source of a refusal, maps to one member and keeps its own vocabulary, residual and re-entry law. stop hold -> indeterminate: deciding evidence not yet admitted (missing seed, unresolved cause or scope, stale frontier or currentness, unobserved state) while every required relation and capability is declared and was exercised; its residual names the evidence that would decide. stop gap -> invalid_basis: a reference or lineage member the judgment requires is unresolved, so the closure it rests on is undefined on this basis until repaired. stop refusal -> falsified for a refusal in the constraint table (the stop of a falsified constraint, as construction availability maps refused to falsified); invalid_basis for a structural refusal. stop invalid_basis -> invalid_basis. stop out_of_scope -> falsified: emitted only with refusal when event_scope_compatible is falsified by a declared scope that conflicts with the admitting authority; an event scope not yet declared is hold plus unresolved_scope, and the spelling is not the core's out_of_frame. stop partial_effect -> falsified: the observed post-state defeats the intended outcome as a whole. transition_evaluation partial -> falsified (the ground of partial_effect). transition_evaluation refused -> falsified, or invalid_basis after a structural refusal. Remaining judgment decisions: the first decision each judgment lists -> satisfied; refused, out_of_scope, unauthorized and stale -> falsified (refused -> invalid_basis after a structural refusal); hold -> indeterminate; gap -> invalid_basis; invalid_basis and transition_evaluation's falsified and indeterminate -> the members so named. A traversal record's stop_states may refine which of hold, gap and refusal map to which non-satisfied member, only by a declared binding giving direction, preserved meaning, narrowed scope and invalidation; other stops, every judgment decision, and any record declaring no binding take the default. A judgment decision is the evaluation's result and a stop is the step's: where values emitted together map to different members (stale with hold), each answers its own question.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:construction-availability-maps-to-core","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:support"},{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:refinements-map-totally-to-core","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:support"}],"clause_type":"relation","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:maps-to","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:traversal-stops-and-decisions-map-to-core"},{"arguments":[{"ref":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:symbol:occurrence-typed-lineage","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:subject"},{"literal":"The wider typed-lineage graph may lawfully contain opposing edges of different kinds: one event may support another while the second corrects the first. A cycle in the untyped union is not a causal cycle unless one declared causal subgraph itself cycles.","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:requirement"},{"literal":"TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE, Causality and typed lineage; P3","role":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:role:qualification"}],"clause_type":"constraint","operator":"urn:stdo-3:claude:vocabulary:operator:distinguishes","uri":"urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:typed-lineage-cycle-is-judged-per-causal-subgraph"}],"closure":{"clauses":["urn:stdo-3:claude:a-c-text:clause:causa